<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Obrenović dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1815">1815</a>–<a href="/years/1903">1903</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as House of Obrenović</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Obrenović dynasty ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> from 1815 to 1842 and again from 1858 to 1903. Founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milos-obrenovic">Miloš Obrenović</a>, leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-serbian-uprising">Second Serbian Uprising</a>, it competed for the throne with the Karađorđević family. Under it Serbia won autonomy and then independence, and became a kingdom in 1882. The line ended when King Alexander and Queen Draga were murdered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/may-coup-serbia">May Coup</a> of 1903.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/obrenovic-dynasty.jpg" alt="Obrenović dynasty" width="320" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: House of Obrenović · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Veliki_dvorski_grb_Obrenovi%C4%87a.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The House of Obrenović, or Obrenović dynasty, traced its male line to Baba Višnja (1737–1817). Its progenitor in power was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milos-obrenovic">Miloš Obrenović</a>, who led the Serbian Uprising of 1815–1817 against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. The uprising led to the formation of the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> in 1817. Like Montenegro, and unlike <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, Serbia did not import a member of an existing European royal family to take its throne. The Obrenović family, like its rivals the Karađorđević, was indigenous.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The dynasty ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> from 1815 to 1842 and again from 1858 to 1903. It was traditionally allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> and opposed the House of Karađorđević, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> supported. Its rulers included Prince Miloš I (1783–1860), his sons Prince Milan II (1819–1839) and Prince Mihailo III (1823–1868), and in the later line King Milan I (1854–1901) and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> (1876–1903). Alexander was the last of the family to reign.</p><p>Its rule ended in a coup d'état by military conspirators, often known today as the Black Hand. They invaded the royal palace and murdered King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a>, who died without an heir. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> then invited Peter Karađorđević to become king of Serbia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Unlike most European dynasties, which used regnal numbers to tell apart rulers who shared a given name, the Obrenović dynasty gave each ruling prince the next number in a single series. There was therefore never a Milan I, a Milan III, a Michael I or a Michael II: Milan II and Michael III were simply the second and third ruling princes of the house. The practice was discontinued when Prince Milan IV proclaimed himself king and declared the principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> a kingdom in 1882. Ljubica Vukomanović was the wife of Prince Miloš I and mother of Prince Milan II and Prince Michael III.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Obrenović” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom grew out of the Principality of Serbia, created by the Serbian revolution of 1804–1817 under Karađorđe and then Miloš Obrenović, and ruled from 1817 by the <b>Obrenović dynasty</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-serbia">Kingdom of Serbia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Power swung between <b>Obrenović</b> princes and Alexander Karađorđević, who reigned from 1842 to 1858.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-serbia">Principality of Serbia</a></li></ul></section>
